<interrogationes unitas="entitas">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I create an entity and set and read its properties?
    <responsum symbolum="entitas_creare entitas_proprietas_ponere entitas_proprietas_capere chorda_internare">entitas_creare(piscina,
      id, genus) with chorda* that are already interned, then
      entitas_proprietas_ponere(e, clavis, valor) (an existing key is
      overwritten and its parse cache reset) and
      entitas_proprietas_capere(e, clavis), which returns the stored
      chorda* or NIHIL. Lookup is a linear scan. The returned value
      is a chorda, so it is NOT NUL-terminated.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does entitas_proprietas_capere return NIHIL for a key I know is set?
    <responsum symbolum="entitas_proprietas_capere entitas_nota_habet chorda_internare chorda_internare_ex_literis">Keys,
      tags, relation ids and relation kinds are compared by POINTER,
      not by content: the library assumes every chorda* came from the
      same InternamentumChorda. A key built with chorda_ex_literis, or
      interned in a different table, never matches. Intern it with
      chorda_internare or chorda_internare_ex_literis in the table that
      interned the stored key. Only entitas_titulum_capere and
      entitas_nota_cum_praefixo_habet compare by content.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I read a property as a number, boolean or timestamp?
    <responsum symbolum="entitas_proprietas_capere_s32 entitas_proprietas_capere_s64 entitas_proprietas_capere_f64 entitas_proprietas_capere_b32 entitas_proprietas_capere_tempus">Use
      the typed accessors: they parse the text on demand, cache the
      result in the Proprietas, and return FALSUM if the key is
      missing or the text does not parse. b32 accepts true, false, 1,
      0, verum and falsum (exact case). tempus accepts only a raw
      millisecond integer (no ISO 8601 yet). Known limits: the s32 and
      f64 parsers copy at most 63 and 127 bytes before strtol/strtod,
      so a longer text is judged on its first bytes only; the s64
      parser rejects the most negative s64, -9223372036854775808, as
      overflow.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I store binary data in an entity property?
    <responsum symbolum="entitas_proprietas_ponere_blobum entitas_proprietas_capere_blobum entitas_proprietas_capere">Yes:
      entitas_proprietas_ponere_blobum gzips the bytes with flatura
      into the piscina you pass, and entitas_proprietas_capere_blobum
      inflates them into a piscina. entitas_proprietas_capere on that
      key returns the COMPRESSED bytes. Empty data (mensura 0) is
      refused. The blob mark is stored in the same typus_literalis
      field the parse cache uses: setting the key again with
      entitas_proprietas_ponere, or calling
      proprietas_parsare_ut_typum(prop, TYPUS_CHORDA or TYPUS_NIHIL)
      on it, overwrites the mark, and capere_blobum then returns
      FALSUM.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I add a relation to another entity and list relations of one kind?
    <responsum symbolum="entitas_relatio_addere entitas_relatio_addere_cum_id entitas_relationes_generis_capere Relatio">entitas_relatio_addere
      gives the relation a fresh UUIDv7 id (interned);
      entitas_relatio_addere_cum_id takes an existing id when
      replaying events. entitas_relationes_generis_capere returns a
      NEW Xar of Relatio VALUES (copies, read with
      (Relatio*)xar_obtinere), and an empty Xar when none match -
      despite its header comment, which says Xar of Relatio* and NIHIL
      when none.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I rely on the order of properties, relations or tags after a delete?
    <responsum symbolum="entitas_proprietas_delere entitas_relatio_delere entitas_nota_delere">No.
      All three deletes are swap-and-pop: the last element is copied
      into the deleted slot and the Xar is truncated. Order is
      insertion order only until the first delete. A Proprietas* or
      Relatio* you held to the deleted slot now sees the moved
      element, and one to the old last element points past
      xar_numerus. Adding never moves elements (Xar segments are
      stable).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I check whether an entity has any tag in a namespace like "#project::"?
    <responsum symbolum="entitas_nota_cum_praefixo_habet entitas_nota_addere entitas_nota_habet">entitas_nota_cum_praefixo_habet(e,
      "#project::") compares the bytes of every tag against a C
      string prefix, so it needs no interning. entitas_nota_habet
      tests one exact tag by pointer, and entitas_nota_addere does
      nothing (and returns VERUM) if that pointer is already
      there.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I get a display name for an entity?
    <responsum symbolum="entitas_titulum_capere">entitas_titulum_capere
      returns the value of the property "name", else "title", else the
      entity id. These two keys are matched by CONTENT, unlike every
      other lookup. It returns the stored chorda* itself, not a
      copy.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I see which type a property was last parsed as, or parse it by a type name?
    <responsum symbolum="entitas_proprietas_capere_plena Proprietas proprietas_parsare_ut_typum typus_literalis_ex_chorda">entitas_proprietas_capere_plena
      returns the Proprietas* itself: typus_literalis and
      parsitus_validus tell what the cache holds (TYPUS_NIHIL and
      FALSUM after a fresh entitas_proprietas_ponere; TYPUS_BLOBUM
      for a blob). To parse by name, map the text with
      typus_literalis_ex_chorda ("chorda", "s32", "s64", "f64", "b32",
      "tempus", and also "blobum", which the header omits; anything
      else gives TYPUS_NIHIL) and call proprietas_parsare_ut_typum.
      It caches on success and returns FALSUM on a parse failure, and
      also for TYPUS_BLOBUM, which it does not handle.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
